1
00:00:28,929 --> 00:00:31,348
So, this is where you've
been hiding, El, huh?

2
00:00:31,390 --> 00:00:33,308
[EL]
Mm.

3
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:35,853
[RICHARD] I was calling for
you just now. Didn't you hear?

4
00:00:35,895 --> 00:00:39,815
I couldn't answer you.
Things are about to get good.

5
00:00:39,857 --> 00:00:43,318
What are we gonna do about this
voracious appetite for books?

6
00:00:44,987 --> 00:00:48,281
[RICHARD] Let's see.
What's the latest distraction?

7
00:00:49,950 --> 00:00:53,037
[RICHARD]
A Sherlock Holmes mystery, huh.

8
00:00:53,078 --> 00:00:54,997
I never could quite
understand the appeal

9
00:00:55,039 --> 00:00:56,999
behind these detective stories.

10
00:00:57,041 --> 00:00:59,001
[EL grunts]

11
00:01:01,378 --> 00:01:04,464
[NARRATOR] Our story is
about the man who managed to

12
00:01:04,506 --> 00:01:08,969
confound even the
world's greatest detective.

13
00:01:14,266 --> 00:01:16,769
[NARRATOR]
The genius criminal mastermind

14
00:01:16,810 --> 00:01:20,439
known as Professor Moriarty.

15
00:03:11,717 --> 00:03:16,262
[RODDY panting]

16
00:03:31,153 --> 00:03:33,155
[RODDY yelps]

17
00:03:39,327 --> 00:03:42,288
[RODDY panting]

18
00:03:52,132 --> 00:03:53,675
[RODDY yelps]

19
00:04:02,517 --> 00:04:06,688
[RODDY whimpers]

20
00:04:17,365 --> 00:04:20,827
[RODDY whimpers]

21
00:04:22,246 --> 00:04:25,207
[RODDY whimpers]

22
00:04:25,249 --> 00:04:27,584
[chuckles]

23
00:04:47,938 --> 00:04:50,690
[WILLIAM] Now there's
been a seventh victim.

24
00:04:50,732 --> 00:04:53,944
"The townsfolk tremble at
the faceless assailant."

25
00:04:53,986 --> 00:04:55,528
Thoughts?

26
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:01,160
A dreadful affair,
William. Simply awful.

27
00:05:01,201 --> 00:05:02,202
Indeed.

28
00:05:02,244 --> 00:05:05,080
They target young boys
for their amusement.

29
00:05:05,122 --> 00:05:07,373
And afterwards, slaughter them.

30
00:05:08,374 --> 00:05:11,377
It's hard to believe
a human could do that.

31
00:05:13,546 --> 00:05:16,716
Most people don't want to
think a man capable of this.

32
00:05:16,758 --> 00:05:19,178
It's a natural response.

33
00:05:21,138 --> 00:05:22,681
"With the culprit
still at large,

34
00:05:22,722 --> 00:05:26,101
the public grows dissatisfied
with law enforcement."

35
00:05:26,143 --> 00:05:27,435
According to this,

36
00:05:27,477 --> 00:05:30,230
there're no clues for
the police to follow.

37
00:05:30,272 --> 00:05:31,731
Oh, but there are clues.

38
00:05:31,773 --> 00:05:33,317
[LOUIS grunts]

39
00:05:33,358 --> 00:05:36,195
[WILLIAM]
Some within this very article.

40
00:05:39,281 --> 00:05:40,782
[WILLIAM]
Looking at the child victims

41
00:05:40,824 --> 00:05:43,660
and the occupations
of their parents,

42
00:05:43,702 --> 00:05:48,748
do you notice the
emergence of a pattern?

43
00:05:48,790 --> 00:05:56,422
[LOUIS] We have a clock smith,
a hatter, a jeweller,

44
00:05:56,464 --> 00:06:01,303
a horse keeper, then a tailor.

45
00:06:01,345 --> 00:06:02,346
[WILLIAM]
And whilst the father

46
00:06:02,387 --> 00:06:04,973
of the latest victim's
business is unknown,

47
00:06:05,015 --> 00:06:08,185
the deceased was
a messenger boy.

48
00:06:08,227 --> 00:06:12,022
People aren't taken in by the
things outside of their view.

49
00:06:12,064 --> 00:06:16,151
Most of us tend to
covet what we've seen.

50
00:06:16,193 --> 00:06:19,779
For example, the omelettes
you make are exquisite.

51
00:06:19,821 --> 00:06:21,281
Even when I'm working
in the field,

52
00:06:21,323 --> 00:06:24,868
they're the first thing
I crave when hunger sets in.

53
00:06:24,910 --> 00:06:27,662
Really? I'm touched.

54
00:06:27,704 --> 00:06:30,498
Now, look. There.

55
00:06:30,540 --> 00:06:31,958
There are people
across the globe

56
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,252
that likely have no knowledge

57
00:06:33,293 --> 00:06:36,004
that a dish called
an omelette even exists.

58
00:06:36,046 --> 00:06:37,130
Nor do I suspect

59
00:06:37,172 --> 00:06:40,425
they'd have the
appetite for one.

60
00:06:40,466 --> 00:06:42,386
Those occupations you listed--

61
00:06:42,427 --> 00:06:44,054
the one element they all share

62
00:06:44,096 --> 00:06:45,972
is that they frequently
find themselves

63
00:06:46,014 --> 00:06:48,641
at the service of
nobles and aristocrats.

64
00:06:48,683 --> 00:06:50,352
[LOUIS gasps]

65
00:06:50,394 --> 00:06:52,187
When a nobleman purchases meat,

66
00:06:52,229 --> 00:06:53,813
he'll send a servant
to the butcher shop

67
00:06:53,855 --> 00:06:55,941
before ever going himself.

68
00:06:55,982 --> 00:06:57,359
That said...

69
00:06:57,401 --> 00:07:00,570
He will visit shops to purchase
his jewellery and clothing.

70
00:07:00,612 --> 00:07:01,654
Even the stables,

71
00:07:01,696 --> 00:07:04,366
because he has to board
his carriage there.

72
00:07:04,408 --> 00:07:06,993
[WILLIAM] Naturally.
Providing opportunities

73
00:07:07,035 --> 00:07:10,038
to encounter members
of their families.

74
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:12,291
It's not unusual
for a shopkeeper's son

75
00:07:12,332 --> 00:07:16,253
to serve as an apprentice
for his father's business.

76
00:07:16,295 --> 00:07:17,754
[LOUIS]
Nor would it be unusual to

77
00:07:17,796 --> 00:07:21,425
encounter messenger boys
at prestigious clubs.

78
00:07:21,466 --> 00:07:24,386
[WILLIAM] Indeed.
Whoever the murderer is,

79
00:07:24,428 --> 00:07:28,265
he never has to wander far in
order to find his next victim.

80
00:07:28,307 --> 00:07:30,100
He's successfully
fed his appetite

81
00:07:30,142 --> 00:07:33,937
on those he comes across
within his social sphere.

82
00:07:33,979 --> 00:07:38,066
We covet what we see every day.

83
00:07:38,108 --> 00:07:39,234
Had it been a few victims,

84
00:07:39,276 --> 00:07:42,904
a coincidence would have
been feasible. Not now.

85
00:07:42,946 --> 00:07:44,697
After five bodies,

86
00:07:44,739 --> 00:07:48,660
his modus operandi has
become all too apparent.

87
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:52,289
[LOUIS grunts]
You mean...?

88
00:07:52,331 --> 00:07:53,332
I think it's about time

89
00:07:53,373 --> 00:07:56,501
we involved ourselves
in this case, Louis.

90
00:07:59,879 --> 00:08:01,340
[LOUIS]
It's odd.

91
00:08:01,381 --> 00:08:03,967
There is one victim that
doesn't fit the profile.

92
00:08:04,009 --> 00:08:06,052
An orphan who
slept on the streets.

93
00:08:06,094 --> 00:08:07,512
What of him?

94
00:08:07,553 --> 00:08:08,847
It is odd.

95
00:08:08,888 --> 00:08:10,182
Noblemen and orphans--

96
00:08:10,223 --> 00:08:14,519
two positions in society
that couldn't be further apart.

97
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:16,896
But they met.

98
00:08:16,938 --> 00:08:18,523
The outlier might
just provide us

99
00:08:18,564 --> 00:08:21,567
with the clue we need
to solve this mystery.

100
00:08:43,048 --> 00:08:45,175
[door opens]

101
00:08:45,217 --> 00:08:46,385
[EDEN]
Lord Albert.

102
00:08:46,426 --> 00:08:48,345
I beg your pardon.

103
00:08:48,387 --> 00:08:50,847
A pleasure to see you
in my shop, as always.

104
00:08:50,889 --> 00:08:53,392
What might I be able
to help you with today?

105
00:08:53,433 --> 00:08:55,018
The tuxedo you ordered?

106
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:57,603
Unfortunately, it isn't
quite finished yet.

107
00:08:57,645 --> 00:09:00,940
[ALBERT] No. That's not
the reason for my visit.

108
00:09:00,982 --> 00:09:02,025
Oh?

109
00:09:02,067 --> 00:09:04,986
Condolences for the
fate that befell Roddy.

110
00:09:05,028 --> 00:09:08,740
[grunts]

111
00:09:11,993 --> 00:09:15,580
If you had let me know, I would
have attended his funeral.

112
00:09:15,621 --> 00:09:17,957
Out of the question.
An earl such as yourself,

113
00:09:17,999 --> 00:09:19,751
coming to a funeral
for commoners?

114
00:09:19,792 --> 00:09:21,711
It'd be undignified!

115
00:09:21,753 --> 00:09:24,464
Imagine what people would say.

116
00:09:26,925 --> 00:09:29,135
[ALBERT]
You're looking a bit careworn.

117
00:09:29,177 --> 00:09:32,264
Shouldn't you be
getting some rest?

118
00:09:32,305 --> 00:09:34,724
I appreciate
your concern, truly.

119
00:09:34,766 --> 00:09:37,102
You see, work is one
of the few retreats

120
00:09:37,143 --> 00:09:39,354
from this overwhelming grief.

121
00:09:40,355 --> 00:09:43,149
[ALBERT] I saw him here in
the store a number of times.

122
00:09:43,191 --> 00:09:45,277
Roddy was a good lad.

123
00:09:45,318 --> 00:09:47,070
[EDEN]
He was.

124
00:09:47,112 --> 00:09:49,489
Forgive a father
and his reminiscing,

125
00:09:49,531 --> 00:09:53,159
but there wasn't a more
obedient or dutiful boy.

126
00:09:53,201 --> 00:09:55,745
For him to meet
such a gruesome end,

127
00:09:55,787 --> 00:10:00,500
it makes you question if there's
true justice in this world.

128
00:10:00,542 --> 00:10:01,418
I wonder...

129
00:10:01,460 --> 00:10:02,877
[ALBERT]
Hm?

130
00:10:05,505 --> 00:10:09,259
[EDEN] ...what I'd do
to the one who did this.

131
00:10:32,949 --> 00:10:35,994
[BOY 1A grunts]

132
00:10:37,454 --> 00:10:39,998
[grunting]

133
00:10:40,039 --> 00:10:41,207
[accordion groans]

134
00:10:41,249 --> 00:10:43,209
[sighs]

135
00:10:48,047 --> 00:10:51,717
[gasps, chuckles]

136
00:10:51,759 --> 00:10:55,388
If you have a moment,
I'd like to have a word.

137
00:10:55,430 --> 00:10:57,641
You don't mind, do you?

138
00:11:07,442 --> 00:11:10,278
[BOY 1A] Sorry for how I'm
lackin' in table manners, sir.

139
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:12,572
So, what is it
you wanted to ask me?

140
00:11:12,614 --> 00:11:15,408
[WILLIAM] That concertina didn't
always belong to you, did it?

141
00:11:15,450 --> 00:11:17,035
[BOY 1A gulps]

142
00:11:19,371 --> 00:11:21,289
Even though it's
your livelihood,

143
00:11:21,331 --> 00:11:24,125
you're not particularly skilled
when it comes to playing it.

144
00:11:24,167 --> 00:11:27,795
Uh! You're not the police
or something, are you?

145
00:11:27,837 --> 00:11:30,256
If I were, would I have
gone through the trouble of

146
00:11:30,298 --> 00:11:32,258
bringing you to this place?

147
00:11:33,301 --> 00:11:34,636
[BOY 1A]
You got a point.

148
00:11:34,678 --> 00:11:36,012
A real bobby woulda
hauled me down

149
00:11:36,054 --> 00:11:40,475
to the station
without sayin' a word.

150
00:11:40,517 --> 00:11:42,268
That 'ol thing used
to belong to Cass.

151
00:11:42,310 --> 00:11:44,479
[WILLIAM gasps]

152
00:11:44,521 --> 00:11:46,189
[BOY 1A] You've heard
about the murders, right?

153
00:11:46,231 --> 00:11:47,566
Don't know why they chose him,

154
00:11:47,607 --> 00:11:49,775
but he was killed
like the others.

155
00:11:49,817 --> 00:11:51,486
Anyway, I was his mate.

156
00:11:51,528 --> 00:11:56,074
So, it only seemed fittin'
that I inherit his business.

157
00:11:56,115 --> 00:11:57,659
Had to relocate though,

158
00:11:57,701 --> 00:12:00,620
seeing how that
corner was cursed.

159
00:12:00,662 --> 00:12:01,663
On that note,

160
00:12:01,705 --> 00:12:04,082
where exactly did
Cass routinely perform?

161
00:12:04,123 --> 00:12:05,166
[BOY 1A]
Hm?

162
00:12:05,208 --> 00:12:07,669
Why? There's nothin' there.

163
00:12:07,711 --> 00:12:11,464
But if you really wanna go,
head two streets over.

164
00:12:18,221 --> 00:12:20,599
[WILLIAM]
Was he spotted from a carriage?

165
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:23,643
No. The boy was chosen as prey.

166
00:12:23,685 --> 00:12:26,979
Meaning the hunter had the
opportunity to study him.

167
00:12:29,107 --> 00:12:31,025
[WILLIAM grunts]

168
00:12:37,323 --> 00:12:41,286
[LOUIS] Then, the killer
saw him from the window?

169
00:12:41,327 --> 00:12:44,830
That is the most
likely scenario, yes.

170
00:12:44,872 --> 00:12:46,416
So, he gazed down at the square,

171
00:12:46,458 --> 00:12:49,711
unable to tear his eyes away
from that unfortunate waif.

172
00:12:49,753 --> 00:12:52,088
And then he struck.

173
00:12:52,130 --> 00:12:54,257
Have you found out
who owns the house?

174
00:12:54,299 --> 00:12:57,636
[WILLIAM] Actually,
it's not a private residence.

175
00:12:57,677 --> 00:13:00,179
The building is where
the Gastros Club meets.

176
00:13:00,221 --> 00:13:03,057
[ALBERT]
The Gastros Club?

177
00:13:03,099 --> 00:13:04,016
That's a place frequented

178
00:13:04,058 --> 00:13:06,645
by wealthy connoisseurs
of good food.

179
00:13:06,686 --> 00:13:09,481
Ah. It sounds as though you're
familiar with them, Albert.

180
00:13:09,522 --> 00:13:12,734
[ALBERT] Oh, just talk,
mostly unflattering.

181
00:13:12,776 --> 00:13:15,403
Still, I do have a
number of acquaintances

182
00:13:15,445 --> 00:13:16,988
who are members there.

183
00:13:17,029 --> 00:13:19,240
That's quite
advantageous for us.

184
00:13:19,282 --> 00:13:24,078
In order to join their circle
a member must refer you.

185
00:13:24,120 --> 00:13:26,872
You think we'll find
him there, don't you?

186
00:13:26,914 --> 00:13:28,207
Indeed.

187
00:13:28,249 --> 00:13:30,835
Considering the number of
indicators pointing us there,

188
00:13:30,876 --> 00:13:33,921
we can't very well
ignore the place.

189
00:13:35,965 --> 00:13:38,677
Well then,

190
00:13:38,718 --> 00:13:42,639
in that case, we may
be able to provide Eden

191
00:13:42,681 --> 00:13:46,225
with a chance for
the vengeance he desires.

192
00:13:55,652 --> 00:13:57,362
[WILLIAM]
Ah, you there.

193
00:13:57,403 --> 00:13:58,780
[SERVER 1A]
Sir, what can I do for you?

194
00:13:58,822 --> 00:14:00,031
Would you mind telling me

195
00:14:00,072 --> 00:14:02,742
who the gentleman is that
always sits by the window?

196
00:14:02,784 --> 00:14:03,868
[SERVER 1A]
Certainly.

197
00:14:03,909 --> 00:14:06,496
That would be
the Earl of Argleton.

198
00:14:06,538 --> 00:14:10,667
He's one of the club's founding
members from what I'm told.

199
00:14:10,709 --> 00:14:13,169
Makes sense. If the man
helped found the place,

200
00:14:13,211 --> 00:14:16,673
then I would say he's free to
reserve any seat he pleases.

201
00:14:16,715 --> 00:14:19,342
Actually, sir, we
have no reservations.

202
00:14:19,384 --> 00:14:22,303
However, I would say
that is his preferred chair

203
00:14:22,345 --> 00:14:24,305
whenever it's available.

204
00:14:29,644 --> 00:14:30,770
[WILLIAM]
Pardon, your lordship.

205
00:14:30,812 --> 00:14:32,731
[grunts]

206
00:14:32,772 --> 00:14:35,233
[WILLIAM]
It is a pleasure to meet you.

207
00:14:35,274 --> 00:14:38,820
My name is William
James Moriarty.

208
00:14:38,862 --> 00:14:41,447
Do we have some sort
of business I'm unaware of?

209
00:14:41,489 --> 00:14:43,241
[WILLIAM]
Hardly that.

210
00:14:43,282 --> 00:14:45,159
There's nothing I require.

211
00:14:45,201 --> 00:14:48,913
See, I noticed you sitting
over here, staring listlessly,

212
00:14:48,954 --> 00:14:51,499
and thought I might engage you
in casual conversation.

213
00:14:51,541 --> 00:14:53,459
Well, despite appearances,
I'm actually--

214
00:14:53,501 --> 00:14:55,795
That is a wonderful suit.

215
00:14:55,837 --> 00:14:58,715
You had it tailored
at Eden's, didn't you?

216
00:14:58,757 --> 00:15:01,551
And that ring is one of a kind.

217
00:15:01,593 --> 00:15:03,636
The mounting is quite elaborate.

218
00:15:03,678 --> 00:15:04,763
If I had to guess,

219
00:15:04,804 --> 00:15:07,932
I'd say the design was
by Goldstein Jewellers.

220
00:15:07,973 --> 00:15:10,351
You're not a detective, are you?

221
00:15:10,393 --> 00:15:12,228
You have quite
the observant eye.

222
00:15:12,270 --> 00:15:14,689
I can go on, if it amuses you.

223
00:15:14,731 --> 00:15:16,733
Based on your
pocket watch chain,

224
00:15:16,775 --> 00:15:19,652
I would say it came from
R. Masters Clock Smith.

225
00:15:19,694 --> 00:15:21,279
Am I correct?

226
00:15:21,320 --> 00:15:22,697
My word.

227
00:15:22,739 --> 00:15:25,199
You deduced that without even
laying eyes on the timepiece.

228
00:15:25,241 --> 00:15:26,242
Incredible.

229
00:15:26,284 --> 00:15:27,660
[WILLIAM]
I haven't seen your hat either,

230
00:15:27,702 --> 00:15:29,954
but I imagine it's
from Morgan Brothers.

231
00:15:29,995 --> 00:15:31,247
Nor your horses,

232
00:15:31,289 --> 00:15:33,499
which are no doubt stabled
at the Shannon Racing Club.

233
00:15:33,541 --> 00:15:35,710
[ARGLETON grunts]

234
00:15:35,752 --> 00:15:39,004
Furthermore, as of late,
your musical interests

235
00:15:39,046 --> 00:15:41,716
have moved towards
the concertina.

236
00:15:44,677 --> 00:15:45,845
[ARGLETON gasps]

237
00:15:45,887 --> 00:15:49,348
And I'll wager you prefer
the meat of young hares.

238
00:15:49,390 --> 00:15:51,810
Tender--not fully developed.

239
00:15:51,851 --> 00:15:55,730
So fresh it practically
drips with blood,

240
00:15:55,772 --> 00:15:58,483
which you take the time
to savour, no doubt.

241
00:15:58,524 --> 00:16:01,277
Nice and slow.

242
00:16:01,319 --> 00:16:03,154
One more observation.

243
00:16:03,195 --> 00:16:05,156
You must be terrible
at playing cards.

244
00:16:05,197 --> 00:16:07,283
[gasps]

245
00:16:07,325 --> 00:16:08,701
Considering how your thoughts

246
00:16:08,743 --> 00:16:14,123
are clearly reflected
on your face.

247
00:16:14,165 --> 00:16:19,211
Excuse me, but there's an
urgent matter I must attend to.

248
00:16:41,108 --> 00:16:43,110
[grunts]

249
00:16:48,324 --> 00:16:51,076
You're here to find out
more about me, no doubt.

250
00:16:51,118 --> 00:16:52,537
Or perhaps

251
00:16:52,578 --> 00:16:54,288
to do something more severe.

252
00:16:54,330 --> 00:16:55,957
[growls]

253
00:16:57,082 --> 00:17:00,169
[WILLIAM] Based on that stance,
you're accustomed to fisticuffs.

254
00:17:00,211 --> 00:17:02,630
But it's your brain
that needs exercise.

255
00:17:02,672 --> 00:17:04,215
After all,
this won't be the same

256
00:17:04,256 --> 00:17:06,342
as kidnapping
defenseless children.

257
00:17:06,384 --> 00:17:08,011
[gasps]

258
00:17:08,052 --> 00:17:11,723
What a poker face. Revealing,
much like your master's.

259
00:17:11,764 --> 00:17:13,599
[growls]

260
00:17:18,563 --> 00:17:21,524
There are several questions
I'd like to ask you.

261
00:17:21,566 --> 00:17:25,403
That is, if you
can spare the time.

262
00:17:25,444 --> 00:17:28,031
[door opens]

263
00:17:28,072 --> 00:17:30,074
Welcome to Eden's. [gasps]

264
00:17:30,115 --> 00:17:32,869
Lord Albert! How are you, sir?

265
00:17:32,911 --> 00:17:34,203
[ALBERT] Mister Eden in?

266
00:17:34,245 --> 00:17:35,747
Well, he's in his office, but--

267
00:17:35,788 --> 00:17:37,916
[door opens]

268
00:17:37,957 --> 00:17:39,834
Lord Albert, welcome back.

269
00:17:39,876 --> 00:17:42,253
What brings you to
my shop at such an hour?

270
00:17:42,294 --> 00:17:44,255
I've grown close to someone

271
00:17:44,296 --> 00:17:46,173
and wanted to ask
her out for dinner.

272
00:17:46,215 --> 00:17:49,176
I figured it was best to stop
by and order something new.

273
00:17:49,218 --> 00:17:52,972
It's a rather detailed
request, I'm afraid.

274
00:17:53,014 --> 00:17:54,390
You came to the right place.

275
00:17:54,432 --> 00:17:56,851
Step into my office
and we'll talk about it.

276
00:17:56,893 --> 00:17:59,103
If you're finished out here,
feel free to go home.

277
00:17:59,144 --> 00:18:01,146
[EMPLOYEE 1A]
Thank you, sir.

278
00:18:36,849 --> 00:18:38,643
[ARGLETON grunts]

279
00:18:40,853 --> 00:18:42,563
[WILLIAM]
Apologies, your lordship.

280
00:18:42,605 --> 00:18:45,066
I admit these methods
are somewhat draconian,

281
00:18:45,108 --> 00:18:47,777
but I doubt you'd
have met me voluntarily.

282
00:18:47,819 --> 00:18:49,236
Slight correction.

283
00:18:49,278 --> 00:18:51,656
It's actually this gentleman
who was adamant about

284
00:18:51,697 --> 00:18:54,700
arranging some time
alone with you.

285
00:18:56,953 --> 00:19:00,081
It's been far too long, my lord.

286
00:19:00,123 --> 00:19:02,500
[ARGLETON yelps]

287
00:19:05,461 --> 00:19:06,754
[EDEN]
Sir William.

288
00:19:06,796 --> 00:19:10,215
I want your guarantee that
you can supply me what I want.

289
00:19:10,257 --> 00:19:13,260
Give me your word!

290
00:19:13,302 --> 00:19:15,013
Mister Eden.

291
00:19:15,054 --> 00:19:17,348
In this twisted
world we live in,

292
00:19:17,389 --> 00:19:20,101
the thing you're proposing,
no matter how justified,

293
00:19:20,143 --> 00:19:23,688
is still considered a crime
in the eyes of the law.

294
00:19:23,729 --> 00:19:27,066
Even so, are you committed
to going through with it?

295
00:19:44,375 --> 00:19:46,293
[EDEN]
Mm.

296
00:19:46,335 --> 00:19:48,171
[WILLIAM]
Very well then.

297
00:19:49,839 --> 00:19:53,134
Leave it to me.
I've accepted your request.

298
00:19:53,176 --> 00:19:57,221
Crime consultant William
James Moriarty is on the case.

299
00:20:00,933 --> 00:20:02,894
[ARGLETON yelps]

300
00:20:05,229 --> 00:20:09,274
Please be quick about it.
I only have one cigarette on me.

301
00:20:24,082 --> 00:20:29,128
[bell tolling]

302
00:20:50,108 --> 00:20:50,942
[WILLIAM]
Mister Eden?

303
00:20:50,983 --> 00:20:52,651
[EDEN grunts]

304
00:20:52,693 --> 00:20:55,487
Come. I think we should be off.

305
00:22:43,512 --> 00:22:46,598
[LOUIS] Beautiful morning,
isn't it, William?

306
00:22:48,851 --> 00:22:51,687
Are there any interesting
headlines in today's paper?

307
00:22:51,729 --> 00:22:53,314
[WILLIAM]
Appears not.

308
00:22:53,356 --> 00:22:56,441
No mention of a debaucherous
earl, his coachman,

309
00:22:56,483 --> 00:23:00,071
or the discovery of their
brutally mutilated bodies.

310
00:23:00,112 --> 00:23:01,446
For the best.

311
00:23:01,488 --> 00:23:05,910
That would certainly set London
hopping, don't you think?

312
00:23:05,951 --> 00:23:08,287
I quite agree.

313
00:23:10,164 --> 00:23:13,251
[WILLIAM] If you remove all
traces of an incident,

314
00:23:13,292 --> 00:23:16,379
then it's as if it never
occurred in the first place.

315
00:23:17,713 --> 00:23:19,590
In other words...

316
00:23:22,134 --> 00:23:24,678
...you commit the perfect crime.

317
00:23:34,146 --> 00:23:37,108
[WILLIAM] Next time on
Moriarty the Patriot  .

318
00:23:37,149 --> 00:23:39,610
"The Scarlet Eyes. Act One."
